<h2 id="three-measures">The three measures</h2>

<p>{{ ("tl-definition" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("tl-definition") }}</p>

<dl>
  <dt>Prices</dt>
  <dd>The rise in the Consumer Prices Index over the 12 months to September.</dd>
  <dt>Earnings</dt>
  <dd>The rise in average weekly earnings across the whole economy, for total pay including bonuses. It compares May to July with the same three months a year earlier.</dd>
  <dt>2.5%</dt>
  <dd>A fixed minimum. It decides the increase when both prices and earnings rise by less than 2.5%.</dd>
</dl>

<p>The triple lock applies to the full rate of the new State Pension and to the basic State Pension. It does not apply to a protected payment, to Additional State Pension or to extra pension from deferring. <a href="/state-pension/#components">Parts of the pension that rise by a different rule</a>.</p>

<h2 id="timing">When the figures are measured and when the pension rises</h2>

<ol>
  <li><strong>May to July.</strong> The period for the earnings figure.</li>
  <li><strong>September.</strong> The end of the 12 months for the prices figure.</li>
  <li><strong>September and October.</strong> The Office for National Statistics publishes the earnings figure in mid-September and revises it in mid-October. It publishes the September prices figure in mid-October. The decision has used the revised earnings figure.</li>
  <li><strong>Autumn.</strong> The Secretary of State announces the increase. In recent years this has been in late October or November.</li>
  <li><strong>The following April.</strong> The new rates take effect in the first full week of the tax year.</li>
</ol>

<p>So the increase paid from April reflects prices and earnings from the year before. When inflation is rising quickly, the pension follows some months behind. When inflation is falling, the increase can be higher than inflation at the time it is paid.</p>

