      <dt>Earnings, May to July before, as used in the decision</dt> <dd>{{ latest.earnings_tenths | percent }}</dd>
      <dt>Minimum</dt> <dd>2.5%</dd>
      <dt>Weekly rate before</dt> <dd>{{ before.new_pence | money }}</dd>
      <dt>Weekly rate after, as published</dt> <dd>{{ latest.new_pence | money }}</dd>
      <dt>Weekly increase</dt> <dd>{{ (latest.new_pence - before.new_pence) | money }}</dd>
      <dt>52 weeks at the new rate</dt> <dd>{{ (latest.new_pence * 52) | money }}</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>These are the published decision and the published rates. This tool did not calculate them.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>How the tool calculates a hypothetical result</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Increase = the highest of prices, earnings and 2.5%. If two or three are equal highest, the result names each of them.</li>
    <li>New amount = amount before × (1 + increase ÷ 100).</li>
    <li>The new amount is rounded to the nearest 5p. An exact half rounds up.</li>
    <li>52-week equivalent = rounded weekly amount × 52.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The tool does not calculate past decisions. It reads them from the published record, because the figure used at the time can differ from the figure published now, and because some years had special rules. <a href="/sources-and-method/#models">Full method and limits</a>.</p>

