<h2 id="compounding">Why one year's increase affects every later year</h2>

<p>Each increase is applied to the rate that the previous increase produced. A higher increase in one year raises the starting point for every later year.</p>

<p>This is also why the triple lock tends to raise the pension faster than either prices or earnings over a long period. In a year when prices rise faster than pay, the pension follows prices. In the next year, if pay catches up, the pension follows pay, from the higher starting point. The Pensions Commission calls this a ratchet. {{ m.c("tl-ratchet") }}</p>

<p>How often does this happen? {{ ("tl-non-earnings-triggered" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("tl-non-earnings-triggered") }}</p>

<p>The <a href="/reform-options/#compare">rule comparison</a> shows the effect under different conditions, including conditions where the rules give the same result.</p>

