<h2 id="limits">What the rule protects against, and its limits</h2>

<p>The rule means the full rate does not fall behind the official measure of prices, or behind average pay, from one year to the next. The 2.5% minimum means it rises in every year.</p>

<p>It has limits.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>A national price index is not each person's bills.</strong> A household that spends more of its money on energy or rent can face a different rate of price rises. <a href="/living-on-a-pension/#prices">Prices and pensioner households</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>The adjustment is yearly and looks back.</strong> It cannot follow prices as they change during the year.</li>
  <li><strong>It raises the rate, not entitlement.</strong> A person who receives less than the full rate gets the same percentage on a smaller amount.</li>
  <li><strong>It does not cover every part of a payment.</strong> Some parts rise with prices only.</li>
  <li><strong>It is a commitment, not law.</strong> A government can change it. On {{ ("pm-care-2026-09-29" | sourceRecord).published_date | timeTag | safe }} the government said it will, from April 2030.</li>
</ul>

<p>Whether the rule should stay is a separate question. <a href="/reform-options/">Reform options</a> sets out the evidence on each side.</p>
